{#
  Homepage running order.

  The rule here is that the offer comes before the argument for it. Previously
  a marquee, the YouTube showcase and the full-height ESP demo were all
  injected between the hero and the products grid, which put the only thing
  actually for sale at y=3558 — four screens down a fourteen-screen page
  carrying under a thousand words. A visitor had to scroll past three
  atmosphere sections before learning what was sold or what it cost.

  Now: hero, then the product, then the evidence for it, then the reviews,
  then the second product, then questions and the closing ask.

  Two title-marquees and the home-flow section are no longer rendered. The
  marquees carried no information at all, and home-flow's "instant delivery,
  no waiting on a human" is already said by the hero's trust row and again on
  every product page — it was a full 1092px section restating one line.
  Both files are left in the repo; they are simply not called.
#}
<div class="components">
  {% render_snippet "esp-field.njk" %}
  {% for componentId in components_order %}
    {% render_component componentId %}

    {# Proof follows the offer. The showcase and the ESP demo are the two
       "see it actually working" blocks, and they read far better as an answer
       to a price the visitor has just seen than as a preamble to it. #}
    {% if componentId == 'products' %}
      {% render_snippet "showcase.njk" %}
      {% render_snippet "esp-demo.njk" %}
    {% endif %}

    {# After the reviews: the verifiable claim, then the second product. #}
    {% if 'feedbacks' in componentId %}
      {% render_snippet "trust-pledge.njk" %}
      {% render_snippet "pair-block.njk" %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
</div>
{% render_snippet "home-buybar.njk" %}
{% render_snippet "waypoint-rail.njk" %}

<style>
/* ── Homepage rhythm ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Measured before this existed: 1604px of the homepage was pure section
   padding on desktop, and every block landed within ~100px of 1100px tall
   whatever it contained — the reviews section was 1060px around 654px of
   content, the closing CTA 980px around 263px. Uniform full-screen sections
   read as slow rather than spacious, because the eye gets no signal about
   which parts matter.

   One scale, applied to the top-level blocks only, so each section is as tall
   as the thing inside it. The hero is excluded: its 900px min-height is a
   deliberate full-viewport opening.

   Longhands, not the `padding` shorthand — several of these sections set
   horizontal padding in the same rule and it must survive. */
.components > *:not(.hero) {
  padding-top: 72px;
  padding-bottom: 72px;
}

/* The closing CTA's own rule already sets its bottom spacing and the card
   carries generous internal padding; adding the rhythm on top double-spaced
   it. */
.components > .cta-section {
  padding-top: 0;
  padding-bottom: 56px;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  /* 128px of top padding on a 393px-wide screen is a third of a viewport
     spent on nothing. The comparison and FAQ sections were doing exactly
     that — their padding never had a mobile step at all. */
  .components > *:not(.hero) {
    padding-top: 44px;
    padding-bottom: 44px;
  }
  .components > .cta-section {
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 36px;
  }
}
</style>
